                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The concept sounds fucking fantastic on paper: a platformer where the level reacts to music. BRILLIANT! Except then you actually play it. The controls feel like they're being operated by a drunk fucking hamster, the levels become repetitive faster than my brain can scream \"OH FOR FUCK'S SAKE, NOT THIS SHIT AGAIN!\", and somehow the game manages to make music feel completely fucking irrelevant. A music-based game that barely does anything interesting with music. Congratulations. That's like making a cooking simulator where the food is just decorative JPEGs. You can import your own music, which sounds cool as hell...Except your music basically sits in the background going:\n\n🎵 la la la 🎵\n\nwhile the game does whatever the fuck it wants. That's it. Your music isn't meaningfully integrated into the gameplay. It's basically a goddamn Spotify player wearing a platformer's costume. And the levels? REPETITIVE AS FUCK. Same shit. Different arrangement. More traps. More frustration. More me questioning why I haven't uninstalled this bastard yet. And apparently the traps are randomized according to the music, except there doesn't seem to be any fucking discernible pattern to actually understand or anticipate them. So instead of learning the level and getting better, you're basically getting slapped in the face by random bullshit and hoping your character survives. Without power-ups, the game becomes borderline fucking unbearable. And keyboard controls? Jesus fucking Christ. Maybe it's better with a controller, but with a keyboard it feels like I'm trying to perform brain surgery using two rusty spoons. There's a good concept buried somewhere in here. A really good concept. Unfortunately, execution showed up drunk, tripped over the controller, fell down the stairs and somehow shipped the game anyway. Maybe in 2013 I would've looked at this and thought:\n\n\"Hey, that's pretty cool!\"\n\nBut in 2026? Absolutely fucking not. Gaming has moved on. My patience has moved on. 2/10. Good concept. Bad execution. Repetitive levels. Awkward controls. Barely meaningful music integration. Random bullshit masquerading as difficulty. I came here to Rush. Instead I rushed to the Uninstall button.",
